WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:02.930
Narrator: Are you sick?

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.190
Don't you dare sneeze on me.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.393
Do you know how far your germs can travel?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.400
Normally you hold about
half a liter of fluid

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.700
in your lungs, so when you sneeze,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.010
you're able to spray as much
as a water bottle's worth

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.870
of mucus into the air around you.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.210
It shoots out of your mouth

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.004
in the form of moist, germ-infested clouds

00:00:25.004 --> 00:00:27.773
traveling up to 35 meters per second.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.640
That's fast enough to
give you a speeding ticket

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.473
on the highway.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.410
Once that cloud hits the air,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.380
it expands, breaking
up into snotty strings,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.657
then a series of different-sized droplets

00:00:39.657 --> 00:00:43.050
all trapped in a turbulent gas cloud.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.830
One brave group at MIT discovered

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:48.260
that the biggest droplets
can travel up to two meters,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.800
about the distance between
your desk and two desks away.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.460
But most other droplets,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.860
those travel much farther,
up to eight meters.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.290
That's about five desks away.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.120
Not only that, the germ-infested gas cloud

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.480
is less dense than the air around it,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.400
so it rises upwards

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.570
right to where ventilation systems

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.933
and other people's unsuspecting
noses and mouths await.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.810
Even though these are
relatively smaller droplets,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.840
they're probably still
teeming with harmful pathogens

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.313
like the flu virus.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.580
Even worse, depending on the ventilation,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.770
they can spread through a
room in just a few seconds

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.478
and remain suspended for up to 10 minutes.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:32.580
Now, if you happen to notice
someone around you sneeze

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.320
or cough, you can hold your breath

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.220
as you move out of range.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.930
But during the flu season,

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.830
that might not even be enough

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.200
because there are danger zones everywhere

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.130
especially on surfaces
like desks, doorknobs

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.540
and steel handrails.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.250
It turns out the flu virus
has been found to survive

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.940
for 24 hours on a steel surface,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.560
and if you touch that surface

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.840
and don't wash your hands before eating,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.760
you may have just gotten infected.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.100
So, let's say you go into
work sick with the flu one day

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.300
and you start to sneeze.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.800
Now, according to one study,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.960
you'll probably only infect
one to two other people.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.980
You're clearly showing
symptoms of the flu.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.540
However, it's gonna take a few days

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.775
for symptoms to show up in
other people you've infected,

00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:22.510
so those poor souls unknowingly
also come into work sick

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.540
over the next few days,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.713
and they will potentially
infect one to two more people.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.670
In an office of 100 desks,

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.990
this could mean 10 or 15 sick people

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.830
by the end of two weeks.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.580
That's over 10% of the office.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.943
And all of it started with
just one sick person. You.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.070
All thanks to the germ-laden cloud

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.053
you so generously shared.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.370
So, the next time you're not feeling great

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.450
and you're sneezing up a storm,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.381
just think about your poor coworkers,

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.693
and take a sick day for
yourself and for them.

